  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 313 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nAs we get into Friday and the weekend, the area will start to see \ninfluence from the ongoing ridge pattern interacting with an \nomega block. The omega block is formed due to a high pressure \nsystem over the Great Lakes, along with low pressure in New \nEngland and the Pacific Northwest. The interaction of these two \nsynoptic features will direct shortwaves over our region acting \nas our source of lift, which will lead to a cloudy, rainy, and \ngloomy weekend. The surface response to the ridging features \nwill cause a sharp temperature gradient over the area, with mid \n80s to our south, and mid to upper 60s to our north. \nAdditionally, high pressure near the Gulf Coast will advect a \nfetch of moisture across the Gulf, and into areas across the \nGreat Plains. Chances of precipitation are expected starting \nFriday morning, with highest likelihood and amounts in our \nnorthwestern counties. Progression of the shortwave eastward \nwill move precipitation chances across the entire area \nthroughout the whole day Saturday. Seasonable temperatures and \npersistent cloud cover significantly reduce instability, and \nlimit severe weather chances. However, higher PWAT values of \n1.5\"-1.75\" indicate that heavy downpours will be possible. Rain \nand cloud cover are expected to diminish Sunday afternoon \nthrough Sunday night as the system continues to progress.\n\nAs the shortwave moves out of the area Monday, the mid level \nridge remains over the area. There is still uncertainty about \nhow high temperatures may get. If the omega block holds onto \nits strength, temperatures will remain seasonable, as it would \nstifle warm air advection. On the other side, if the omega \nblock weakens, this would allow a warmer air mass to move \nfarther northward into our area, bringing temperatures into the \nmid 80s. Ensembles have been leaning toward the omega block \nbreaking down and moving out of the region, making way for \nanother disturbance from the Pacific coast. This system would \ncome through the area Tuesday, bringing increased chances of \nrain, and cooler temperatures.",
